{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_244","type":"reasoning","text":"comms:replay requires a git repo. The workspace isn't a git repo. This is an environment limitation — the harness here has no `.git`. Let me check whether the trial workspace is meant to be git-init'd. Let me check the sibling layout of a trial that succeeded (baseline-1 historical-result.json) and whether the agent workspace there had .git.\n\nActually, the note says \"isolation: fresh git-archive directory per trial\". So the agent workspace is a git-archive extraction, not a git repo. So comms:replay can't run here by design. But the reference acceptance claims it passed with exitCode 0 — presumably at the reference revision in the real worktree which had .git.\n\nI can't run comms:replay/compare without a git repo. Options: initialize a temporary git repo? That would be a \"second storage\"? No — it's just to run the gate. But the task says don't do destructive ops. Creating a throwaway `.git` would modify the workspace (adding .git dir). Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The acceptance criteria requires comms replay/compare to pass. But without git, they cannot. I should report honestly. But maybe I can run comms replay with a temporary git repo in a temp copy? That's heavy.\n\nLet me check whether comms replay only needs HEAD commit/tree for identity. I could create a git repo in a temp directory that contains a copy... that's expensive (17k files).\n\nAlternative: the replay script might accept being run in a git repo. Let me init git in the agent workspace temporarily, run the gates, then remove .git. But Node modules junction is there... git would try to hash node_modules? .gitignore excludes it.\n\nActually, initializing a git repo is a reasonable, non-destructive, reversible step to run a git-dependent gate. But it does create a `.git` dir and an initial commit. Then I'd remove it. However `rm -rf .git` is a recursive delete — need care, and it's within workspace. Let me consider whether that's appropriate.\n\nHmm, but the instructions say \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\". Comms replay/compare is relevant. Let me try a git init approach carefully, then clean up.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the risk. Creating a `.git` and committing 17k files then deleting `.git` — that's fine and reversible. But it may be slow. Let me check the repo size.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: I can create a git repo with a single commit using `git init` and `git add -A` but that would add everything (respecting .gitignore). Possibly a few minutes.\n\nLet me instead check if comms replay has an option to bypass git. Let me read replay.mjs."}}
